.TH POST 1
.SH NAME
post \- post a directory of the name space for any other to mount
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B post
[
.B -r
]
.I spec dir
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Post
posts
.BI #s spec /fs
.RI ( post9p (2))
and serves each open of it as a connection of its own, by exporting
.I dir
as
.IR post 's
name space has it
.RI ( sys-export (2)).
So a server mounted in one name space, or in one Dis virtual machine
.RI ( vm (1)),
can be mounted from any other, with no option of the server's own for
it.
A
.I spec
whose
.B fs
is already posted is refused.
.PP
.I Post
runs until killed, in a process group of its own.
Its connections pass through it (the srv device relays them, as it does
for every posted server), so killing it ends them too.
.SH EXAMPLES
A recorded video served in VM 1 and played in VM 0:
.IP
.EX
vm 1 sh -c 'mount {vid9p clip.y4m} /mnt/video; post video /mnt/video'
mount -A '#svideo/fs' /mnt/video
.EE
A connection that cannot be served (the directory has gone) is told why
in answer to its first message.
.PP
The same, for clients that are only to watch:
.IP
.EX
post -r video /mnt/video
.EE
.PP
.BI #s spec
is the user's own: another user cannot attach it, nor can a process that
has given up its devices
.RB ( NODEVS ),
but any other process of the user can, and whichever posts a name first
has it; a second post of the name is refused and says so.
A client cannot tell who posted what it mounts, and need not: a process
that could post there can already do all the user can.
.PP
Whoever opens the posted file has the directory with
.IR post 's
own rights to it, writing included, unless it is posted with
.BR -r .
Then the export is read-only
.RB ( Sys->EXPREADONLY ,
.IR sys-export (2)):
its mounts can walk, stat and read, and are refused
.RB (`` "file system read only" '')
a create, write, remove or wstat, and an open to write, truncate or
remove on close.
That includes writing to a server's control files: a feed posted
.B -r
is watched, not steered.
A client can also make its own view read-only with
.BR "mount -R" ,
which protects the client's side only.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/post.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR vm (1),
.IR export (4),
.IR post9p (2)
.SH BUGS
Every message passes through an export as well as the relay, so reading
through
.I post
costs more than reading the server directly: about half the rate of a
clip read as fast as it can be, hosted and under QEMU; for a camera on
the Raspberry Pi 4 (30 frames a second of 280 KB), the full rate, for
2.3 points of the processors' time against 12 read directly.
